{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790542804,"nonce":"ZyKZSnvGLNiWX4rlNaCyfYE8sII=","title":"wasm: entering a Haxe try costs about 200 ns, paid when nothing throws","message":"A trap on wasm is hlp_setup_trap_jit plus setjmp under LLVM's wasm SjLj lowering, and its cost is paid at every entry. It dominates the host export thunks (501b924), which put every call inside a trap.\n\nMeasured by caribou on wasm32-wasip1 under wasmtime, 200000 iterations of `s = add(s)`:\n- plain loop: 5.5 ns per iteration\n- inside `try { s = add(s); } catch (e:Dynamic) {}`: 184 ns\n- the same with the fiber transform off: 214 ns, so the transform is not the cause\n- a Wren call into Haxe through an export: 280-310 ns for static, method, getter and setter, against 24-55 ns for Haxe calling Wren\n\nNot yet separated, and worth measuring before choosing a fix:\n- the SjLj lowering's own per-setjmp cost (__wasm_setjmp and its table, and the rewrite of every call in the function);\n- hlp_setup_trap_jit itself (a runtime call, thread-local state);\n- the optimisation shield: a function containing a trap is kept out of the middle end (optnone), so in the benchmark the loop body is unoptimised and `add` is not inlined. Natively the shield is paid too, so this term is not wasm-specific.\n\nDirection: the modules are built with exception-handling, so a trap could lower to a wasm try/catch whose entry costs nothing, with hlp_throw raising a wasm exception tag instead of longjmp. Every Haxe try on wasm benefits, not only the exports. The native targets keep setjmp.","files":null}]}